<%# A `<label for>` *does* name a `<button>` — button is a labelable element,
    and Chrome's accessibility tree gives this switch the name "Airplane mode"
    from the label alone (`relatedElement`, measured). This preview said the
    opposite for as long as it existed, which is the misconception
    decisions/04-bugs-fixed.md exists to correct.

    So the first two follow upstream's own example and use `<label for>`. The
    third and fourth have no visible label at all, which is what `aria-label`
    is for; the FormBuilder wires `aria-labelledby` instead, because it already
    renders the label and knows its id. All three name the control. %>
<div class="flex items-center gap-6">
  <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
    <%= render(Shadcn::Switch::Component.new(name: "airplane", id: "switch-airplane")) %>
    <%= render(Shadcn::Label::Component.new(for: "switch-airplane")) { "Airplane mode" } %>
  </div>
  <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
    <%= render(Shadcn::Switch::Component.new(size: :sm, checked: true, id: "switch-small")) %>
    <%= render(Shadcn::Label::Component.new(for: "switch-small")) { "Small, on" } %>
  </div>
  <%= render(Shadcn::Switch::Component.new(disabled: true, "aria-label": "Disabled")) %>
  <%# Upstream's "Invalid". Added here rather than to a page of its own: it is
      one attribute, and it says more beside the states it is not. %>
  <%= render(Shadcn::Switch::Component.new("aria-invalid": "true", "aria-label": "Invalid")) %>
</div>
